# Scoped deliberately to ONE file. This is not a repo-wide EOL normalization —
# that is `housekeeping/eol-normalize`'s arc, and doing it here would rewrite the
# working copy of every file in every concurrent worktree on this machine.
#
# WHY THIS ONE FILE. `probe-public-surfaces.mjs` is imported by
# `__tests__/unit/routes/marcoPoloProbe.test.ts`. Git stores it with LF, as git
# stores everything; `core.autocrlf=true` on Windows checks it out with CRLF;
# and vitest's ESM loader rejects a CRLF `.mjs` with `SyntaxError: Invalid or
# unexpected token` at the import line. The test then fails with ONE failed FILE
# and ZERO failed tests — a load error, not an assertion — which reads like a
# code defect and is not one. §13.3 Item 268.
#
# It fires on every fresh clone, every new worktree, and every checkout that
# restores the file, so it has cost several sessions a diagnosis each. CI never
# sees it: Linux has no autocrlf, so the file is LF there and the suite is green
# on a tree that is red locally.
#
# `text eol=lf` makes git check this file out with LF whatever autocrlf says,
# which removes the trap rather than asking each session to remember the manual
# `cp` that works around it.
backend/scripts/probe-public-surfaces.mjs text eol=lf

# MIGRATIONS: PRISMA HASHES THESE FILES, SO THEIR LINE ENDINGS ARE PART OF THE
# RECORD. `prisma migrate deploy` stores the SHA-256 of each migration.sql as it
# read it from disk. With autocrlf a Windows working copy is CRLF, so a migration
# applied from a Windows shell stores a CRLF hash while every Linux checkout
# (Render, CI) hashes the LF file git holds. That happened on 2026-09-26 to
# 20260926140000_invoice_delivery_channel, applied through the production rail;
# its stored checksum is the CRLF one and is deliberately left as it is. Deploy
# and status do not compare checksums (tested), but `migrate dev` does, and the
# record should not depend on which machine applied it. eol=lf checks these
# files out with LF whatever autocrlf says.
backend/prisma/migrations/** eol=lf
